 ## Overview
-Dual Rendering Equation Optimization (DREO) is a differentiable volume rendering method that jointly optimizes scene geometry and appearance by minimizing a combined loss derived from two distinct rendering equations: one for volumetric density accumulation and one for surface reflectance [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard NeRF which relies solely on volumetric integration, DREO explicitly models surface normals and BRDF properties to enable relighting [S1, unofficial]. It differs from [Deformable and Dynamic Fields](11311-deformable-and-dynamic-fields.md) by focusing on the dual-equation loss formulation rather than temporal deformation fields, and from [Surface-SDF Representation](10778-surface-sdf-representation.md) by using a neural radiance field tensor optimized via volume rendering integrals rather than explicit signed distance functions.
